# The Myth of the Divided Spring

In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a great mountain. Each year, the Spring of Abundance flowed from the peak, bringing water that could nourish crops or be hoarded to make gold.

The First Village discovered they could send water upstream through carved channels. Whatever they sent would triple as it passed through the mountain's mineral heart. The Second Village would then decide how much water to return downstream.

For generations, fear ruled both sides. The First Village sent nothing, trusting no one. The Second Village never received the chance to prove their honor. Both suffered through droughts, their fields cracked and barren.

Then came the year of desperate hunger. An elder from the First Village said, "We die slowly in certainty, or risk living through trust." They sent half their water upstream.

The mountain tripled it, and the Second Village faced their test. Some voices counseled keeping all—they had received it fairly. But their elder said, "Trust given deserves trust returned."

They sent back more than was originally given.

The next year, both villages sent more. The pattern held. Sometimes miscommunication caused doubt, but the memory of that first reciprocity sustained them.

Both villages flourished, bound not by law, but by the fragile covenant of honored trust.
